**EXERCISE 1.26.** (For algebraists). Let $A$ be a commutative ring with unit. Let $\Delta$ be the set of finite subsets $F \subseteq A$. Define

$$
I(F) = \{G \in \Delta \mid F \subseteq \text{the ideal generated by } G\}.
$$

Prove that the sets of the form $I(F)$ form a neighbourhood system, and that the corresponding domain is isomorphic to the set of ring-theoretic ideals of $A$ partially ordered by inclusion. What would happen if we excluded from $\Delta$ all $F$ with $I(F) = I(\{1\})$, where $1$ is the unit of $A$?

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`IFamily`).** The neighbourhood $I(F)$ consists of those finite sets whose generated ideal contains $F$.

**Theorem (`IFamily_inter`).** The intersection $I(F_1)\cap I(F_2)$ equals $I(F_1\cup F_2)$.

**Theorem (`IFamily_subset_iff`).** Inclusion of neighbourhoods $I(F_1)\subseteq I(F_2)$ is equivalent to $F_2$ lying in the ideal generated by $F_1$.

**Definition (`ringSystem`).** The neighbourhood system of sets of the form $I(F)$ on the finite subsets of $A$.

*Proof.* Master neighbourhood $I(\emptyset)$; intersections via the union identity above. ∎

**Definition (`idealOf`).** From an ideal element, the ring ideal of those $a$ for which $I(\{a\})$ belongs to the filter.

**Definition (`ofIdeal`).** From a ring ideal $J$, the ideal element of those $I(F)$ with the span of $F$ contained in $J$.

**Theorem (`ringIso`).** Ideal elements are order-isomorphic to the ring ideals of $A$ ordered by inclusion.

*Proof.* The two maps are mutually inverse and preserve approximation. ∎

**Theorem (`IFamily_eq_of_span_eq`).** Equal spans yield equal neighbourhoods $I(F)$.

**Theorem (`exercise_1_26`).** Equal spans of finite sets yield equal $I$-neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* The span-equality criterion for $I$-families. ∎
